<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.2.3: Multiplying a Polynomial by a Polynomial</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01585</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.2.3: Multiplying a Polynomial by a Polynomial</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d6a8c0e2-52b3-49f0-b0b8-587f6387c535</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity reinforces the use of Vertical Alignment and distribution to multiply a binomial by a larger polynomial. Emphasize that the FOIL method will only work when you are multiplying two binomials, not with a trinomial or a larger polynomial.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Allow students to work individually on the exercises provided. Allow extra time to answer questions as needed.</para>
<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para><!--Extra Support Name-->Action and Expression:  Flexibility and Choice</para>
  <!--Support Content-->
    <para>Provide students with algebra tiles to help multiplying polynomials.   Algebra tiles help represent algebraic equations and provide students with a visualization of the process of multiplying polynomials and trinomials. </para>
    <para>Watch the video from 6.2.2 above Question 1 for an example of how to use algebra tiles multiplying polynomials.</para>
    <para><emphasis effect="italics"><!--Support Italics--> 
      Supports accessibility for Conceptual Processing, Visual-Spatial Processing </emphasis></para></note>

<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Match each polynomial expression to its simplified solution. Be prepared to explain or show your work.</para>
<list>
  <item> \(c^3-7c^2+11c+3\) </item>
  <item> \(c^3-5c^2-5c-6\) </item>
  <item> \(c^3+7c^2+6c-20\) </item>
  <item>\(c^3+4c^2+9c+6\) </item>
  <item> \(c^3+5c^2-48c+20\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((c+1)(c^2+3c+6)\) </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(c^3+4c^2+9c+6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((c-6)(c^2+c+1)\) </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(c^3-5c^2-5c-6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((c+5)(c^2+2c-4)\) </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(c^3+7c^2+6c-20\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((c^2-3)(c^2-4c-1)\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(c^4-4c^3−4c^2+12c+3 \)</item>
  <item>\(c^4-c^3−4c^2+7c-3 \)</item>
  <item>\(c^4-c^3−2c^2+12c+3 \)</item>
  <item>\(  c^4-7c^3−2c^2+7c+3\) 
  </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(c^4-4c^3−4c^2+12c+3 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((c^2+10)(c^2-5c+2)\) </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(2c^4-5c^3+12c^2-15c+20 \)</item>
  <item>\(c^4-5c^3+8c^2-50c+20 \)</item>
  <item>\(c^4-5c^3-8c^2-50c+20\) </item>
  <item>\(2c^4-5c^3-8c^2-15c+12\)</item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(c^4-5c^3+8c^2-50c+20 \) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Now, fill in the missing blanks to complete the polynomial multiplication.</item>
  <para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}\left(2x-4\right)\left(x^2+\square x-5\right)&amp;=&amp;2x\left(x^2+6x-5\right)-4\left(x^2+6x-\square\right)\\&amp;=&amp;2x^3+\square x^2-10x-4x^2-24x+\square\\&amp;=&amp;\square x^3+8x^2-\square x+20\end{array}\) </para>
</list>
<para>Answer:</para>
<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}\left(2x-4\right)\left(x^2+6x-5\right)&amp;=&amp;2x\left(x^2+6x-5\right)-4\left(x^2+6x-5\right)\\&amp;=&amp;2x^3+12x^2-10x-4x^2-24x+20\\&amp;=&amp;2x^3+8x^2-34x+20\end{array}\)</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>If students are struggling with filling in the blanks to complete the polynomial multiplication, encourage them to use what they know about the Distributive Property to complete the first line. The following lines can be found by continuing to solve by distribution.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students to consider the advantages and disadvantages of each method used in the activity. Consider the following:</para>
<list>
  <item> Using Vertical Alignment allows you to align like terms so that they can be easily combined into the final product. </item>
  <item> How does the time spent using Vertical Alignment compare to the time spent using the Distributive Property? </item>
  <item> Which method do you find easier to use to double-compare your work? </item>
</list>
<title>Video: Multiplying Polynomials Using Different Methods</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to multiply polynomials using both the Distributive Property and Vertical Alignment.</para>
<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/60f331c1efb175db53ec991f134d76cf815be109"/></media><title>6.2.3: Self Check</title>
<!--BEGIN SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->
<para><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<!--SELF CHECK QUESTION GOES BEFORE THE Table -->
<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para>Multiply.</para>
<para>\((d+4)(2d^2-3d+5)\)</para>
<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(2d^3-11d^2-17d+20\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the signs of the terms you found when multiplying and combining terms. The answer is \(2d^3+5d^2-7d+20\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(2d^3+5d^2-7d+20\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Use the Distributive Property or Vertical Alignment to compare your work.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(2d^3-3d^2+5d\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This is only \((d)(2d^2-3d+5)\). Multiply \(2d^2-3d+5\) by 4 and combine like terms of both parts. The answer is \(2d^3+5d^2-7d+20\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(8d^2-12d+20\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This is only \((4)(2d^2-3d+5)\). Multiply \(2d^2-3d+5\) by \(d\) and combine like terms of both parts. The answer is \(2d^3+5d^2-7d+20\).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!--END SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables --> 

<title>6.2.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Multiplying a Polynomial by a Polynomial</title>
<para>We have multiplied monomials by monomials, monomials by polynomials, and binomials by binomials. Now we’re ready to multiply a polynomial by a polynomial. Remember, FOIL will not work in this case, but we can use other representations of the Distributive Property such as Vertical Alignment or distributing individual terms or polynomials.</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>Multiply \((b+3)(2b^2-5b+8)\) using the Distributive Property by distributing either a binomial or an individual term.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis> Distribute the \((b+3)\) to \((2b^2−5b+8)\). </para>
<para> \(b
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}2}
  {\style{color:red}b}
  ^
  {\style{color:red}2}\;     {\style{color:red}-}\;     {\style{color:red}5}
  {\style{color:red}b}\;     {\style{color:red}+}\;     {\style{color:red}8}
  {\style{color:red})}
  +3
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}2}
  {\style{color:red}b}
  ^
  {\style{color:red}2} \;{\style{color:red}-}\;     {\style{color:red}5}
  {\style{color:red}b} \;{\style{color:red}+}\;     {\style{color:red}8}
  {\style{color:red})}
  \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> Multiply.</para>
<para> \(2b^3-5b^2+8b+6b^2-15b+24\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
<para> \(2b^3+b^2-7b+24\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para> Multiply \((b+3)(2b^2-5b+8)\) using vertical alignment. </para>
<para> It is easier to put the polynomial with fewer terms on the bottom because we get fewer partial products this way.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis> Multiply. </para>
<para> \((2b^2-5b+8)\) by \(3\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> Multiply.</para>
<para> \((2b^2-5b+8)\) by \(b\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Add like terms.</para>
<para>\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2b^2-;5b+8\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\times\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;b+3}\\ \; \\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;6b^2-15b+24\\2b^3-5b^2\;+8b}\\\;\\2b^3\;+\;b^2-\;7b+24\)</para>

<title>Try It: Multiplying a Polynomial by a Polynomial</title>
<para>For questions 1 - 2, Multiply \((z-3)(z^2-5z+2)\) using the listed method.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> The Distributive Property</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis> Distribute. </para>
<para> \((z^2 - 5z + 2)\).
  \(z(z^2-5z+2)-3(z^2-5z+2)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> Multiply.</para>
<para> \(z^3-5z^2+2z-3z^2+15z-6\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
<para> \(z^3-8z^2+17z-6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Vertical Alignment</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis> Multiply.</para>
<para>\((z^2-5z+2)\) by \(–3\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> Multiply.</para>
<para>\((z^2-5z+2)\) by \(z\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Add like terms.</para>
<para>\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;z^2-5z+2\\\;\;\;\;\times\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;z-3}\\\;\;\\\;\;\;\;-3z^2+15z-6\\\;z^3-5z^2+2z}\\\;\;\\z^3-8z^2+17z-6\)</para>
</section>
</content>
